    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm trying to set up some reorder logic for our shop. we sell a lot of lithium-ion battery components and sometimes we run out right when we need them most. can you help me figure out a formula for setting safety stock levels so we don't hit zero during peak periods? i have the lead times and average daily usage, but i'm not sure how to account for the variability.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate safety stock, you'll want to consider the variability in your lead times and daily usage, along with the desired service level. Here's a simple formula you can start with: Safety Stock = Z-score x Standard Deviation of Lead Time x Daily Usage / Square root of Day-to-Day Usage. The Z-score corresponds to your desired service level (e.g., 1.65 for 95% service level). This calculation assumes your lead times and daily usage follow a normal distribution, which is an approximation. Keep in mind, factors like seasonal demand impacts and lot sizes, if changed frequently, should also be considered. It might also be helpful to run some simulations based on your actual data.",
      "source": "target",
